The java manual is blunt about it. An AOT cache belongs to one application class path, one JDK release, and one operating system and CPU, and "if any of the above changes, you must recreate the AOT cache". The JVM checks, and in the default auto mode it starts without the cache when a check fails. A changed JAR is the worst case, because on early JDK 25 builds that check was missing. On 25.0.0 to 25.0.3, and on 26.0.0 to 26.0.1, the JVM never compared the application's JARs with the cache (bug JDK-8377932). Rebuild the JAR, keep the old cache, and the JVM serves the old classes. The research behind this course reproduced it on JDK 25 GA. The log said the class came from shared objects file, and the rebuilt program printed version ONE, the text of the build before. A real shopkeeper would notice last week's stock on her shelves. Those builds didn't. The fix shipped in JDK 27 and was backported to 25.0.4 and 26.0.2. StaleCacheDemo, in the Try it below, runs the same experiment. On 25.0.5 the JVM rejects the cache and runs the new code (output trimmed): [0.038s][warning][aot] This file is not the one used while building the AOT cache: 'hello.jar', timestamp has changed, size has changed [0.038s][error ][aot] shared class paths mismatch (hint: enable -Xlog:class+path=info to diagnose the failure) [0.039s][error ][aot] Unable to map shared spaces version TWO, rebuilt So require JDK 25.0.4 or later (26.0.2 or later on 26), and rebuild the cache with every JAR, in the same build step. On a fixed JDK an old cache can't run old code, but it still costs you the whole gain. Here's the surprise the opening promised. A cache the JVM can't use leaves you with no archive at all: unlike AppCDS, it doesn't fall back to the default CDS archive. The one-class program in that demo loaded 542 classes from the default archive with no flag, and 0 with the stale cache. Even the version line shows it: java -XX:AOTCache=nope.aot -version ends with mixed mode, where a normal start says mixed mode, sharing. A typo in the file name makes start-up slower than no flag at all. The garbage collector matters on JDK 25 too. A garbage collector, or GC, is the part of the JVM that frees memory no object uses anymore, and you choose one with a flag. On 25.0.5, a cache trained under the default collector, G1, gave 0 archived classes under -XX:+UseZGC, and all 959 under the Parallel and Serial collectors. JDK 26's JEP 516 removes this limit. Until then, train with the collector you run. Module and agent options must match as well: --module-path, --add-modules and --enable-native-access should be the same in training and production. The manual also lists "certain JVMTI options" as incompatible. JVMTI is the JVM's tool interface. Agents, add-ons loaded into the JVM to watch or change a program, use it to rewrite classes as they load, so this matters the day someone adds a monitoring agent. Two flags catch all of this. -Xlog:aot prints Opened AOT cache app.aot. when the cache works, and the reason when it doesn't. -XX:AOTMode=on (JDK 24 to 26; required from JDK 27) makes the JVM stop with Unable to use AOT cache. instead of running without it. Use that in your build's checks, and keep it out of production. The JDK 27 manual warns that your application "may fail to launch" if, for example, a cloud provider's monitoring agent adds an incompatible option. A cache the JVM can't use is worse than none. Check that it opened, in every build. Check yourself: you train on JDK 25.0.4 with G1, then deploy the same JAR on 25.0.5 with G1. Does the cache load? (No. The cache records the exact JDK build, and the JVM refuses one made by any other build.) Even a cache that opens leaves work in every new JVM: the interpreter still runs first, and the JIT still compiles. The next lesson removes the JVM's start-up altogether, at a price. Run new code with an old cache Read StaleCacheDemo. It compiles a one-class program that prints "version ONE", trains a cache on it, rebuilds the JAR to 