m Lamp's file alone. On a later build, KSP uses that to redo only the cards whose files changed. For now, one sentence is enough: name the file each output came from. Lesson 7 shows what goes wrong when you don't. CardDemo runs the whole lesson 2 processor on two classes in memory, and prints what KSP said and every file it wrote: exit code: OK i: [ksp] l02 round 1: @Inventory on [Lamp, Plant] i: [ksp] l02 round 2: @Inventory on [] --- movemate/LampInventoryCard.kt // Generated by InventoryProcessor from Lamp. Do not edit. package movemate fun Lamp.inventoryCard(): String = "Lamp: name=$name, bulbs=$bulbs, shade=$shade" --- movemate/PlantInventoryCard.kt // Generated by InventoryProcessor from Plant. Do not edit. package movemate fun Plant.inventoryCard(): String = "Plant: kind=$kind, potLitres=$potLitres, waterEveryDays=$waterEveryDays" --- lampCard() returns: Lamp: name=desk lamp, bulbs=1, shade=null Notice the two i: lines at the top: the processor ran twice. KSP calls process once per round, one pass over the files. A round that writes new files earns another round, in which those files are read too, in case they carry sticky notes of their own. Round 2 asked the same question and got [], because the cards carry no @Inventory. Nothing new was written, so processing ended and the compiler took over. The figure draws those two rounds, and lesson 6 makes rounds the whole topic. Here the picture bends. A real helper would notice a new card the moment it was written. KSP's helper sees new files only in the next round, and it's the same helper on every pass: KSP creates the processor once per build and calls process again each round. Rounds set a trap. TwiceDemo has a processor that doesn't ask for annotated symbols. It walks getAllFiles(), every file, in every round, and round 2's files include Lamp.kt again: exit code: INTERNAL_ERROR w: [ksp] round 1: getAllFiles() = [Lamp.kt] w: [ksp] round 2: getAllFiles() = [Lamp.kt, LampInventoryCard.kt] kotlin.io.FileAlreadyExistsException: .../kotlin/movemate/LampInventoryCard.kt The helper cheerfully started Lamp's card a second time, and KSP stopped the whole build. A file may be created only once per build, in KSP as in javac, whose Filer throws a FilerException for the same mistake. getSymbolsWithAnnotation protects you here: in each round it returns only symbols from that round's new files, plus any set aside for later. Write each output once. Ask the resolver for what's new in this round, not for everything. The happy path works. But a sticky note can land on something that isn't a class at all. Print the cards, then write one twice Run CardDemo and read every line it prints. Which line tells you there were two rounds? Run TwiceDemo with ./gradlew :ksp:demo -Pmain=workbench.ksp.l02.demo.TwiceDemoKt. Find the round where it went wrong, and the file it tried to write again. Once your lab passes, run CardDemo again with -Pmine=l02. Your processor should print the same output, line for line. CardDemo.kt package workbench.ksp.l02.demo import com.tschuchort.compiletesting.KotlinCompilation import com.tschuchort.compiletesting.SourceFile import com.tschuchort.compiletesting.configureKsp import com.tschuchort.compiletesting.kspSourcesDir import java.io.File import java.io.OutputStream import workbench.ksp.l02.InventoryProcessorProvider // Rung 3: the whole @Inventory processor on two MoveMate classes, in memory. Prints what KSP said, every file the // processor wrote, and the result of calling one generated card. Reference processor by default; -Pmine=l02 for yours. // ./gradlew :ksp:demo -Pmain=workbench.ksp.l02.demo.CardDemoKt fun main() { val moveMate = SourceFile.kotlin( "MoveMate.kt", """ package movemate import workbench.ksp.annotations.Inventory @Inventory data class Lamp(val name: String, val bulbs: Int, val shade: String?) @Inventory data class Plant(val kind: String, val potLitres: Double, val waterEveryDays: Int = 7) fun lampCard() = Lamp("desk lamp", 1, null).inventoryCard() ""