First, the job. MoveMate is the small app this track builds for the family's move. One of its classes is data class Lamp(val name: String, val bulbs: Int, val shade: String?), marked with the annotation @Builder. An annotation is the sticky note on the box: here it says "make a builder for this one". For each class with that note, our processor should write LampBuilder. It needs one private field per constructor parameter, each starting empty, and one setter per field that returns the builder, so that calls chain. Last comes build(), which calls Lamp's constructor. The quickest way to write that file is lesson 2's way. Build the text line by line, then hand it to KSP's CodeGenerator, the object that creates new files. Java has had annotation processors, programs that run inside the compiler and write new source files, since Java 6. KSP is Kotlin's own way to do that job for Kotlin code, and faster than running Java's processors on Kotlin through kapt. Here is the heart of the template version, next to its closest Java equivalent: Java // Closest Java equivalent: a javac annotation processor that writes text through the Filer JavaFileObject file = processingEnv.getFiler().createSourceFile(pkg + "." + name + "Builder", type); try (Writer out = file.openWriter()) { out.write("package " + pkg + ";

