Jobs a helper can't do: a second Lamp won't compile, a card next to the box will, and three questions pick the tool Lesson 1 named KSP's two limits in the docs' words: processors "can't examine expressions or statements, and they can't modify the source code". A newcomer reads that and thinks of a workaround: if a processor can't add a function to Lamp, it can write Lamp again, with the function inside. RepackDemo tries exactly that, and then the way that works: Java // The javac way, the same as KSP's: a new file next to the class, never a change to it public final class LampCards { private LampCards() {} public static String inventoryCard(Lamp lamp) { return "Lamp: name=" + lamp.getName() + ", bulbs=" + lamp.getBulbs() + ", shade=" + lamp.getShade(); } } Kotlin // What a KSP processor can do: a new file next to the class, with an extension function fun Lamp.inventoryCard(): String = "Lamp: name=$name, bulbs=$bulbs, shade=$shade" // What it can't do: give Lamp itself a new member. Writing Lamp again is a second Lamp. data class Lamp(val name: String, val bulbs: Int, val shade: String?) { fun inventoryCard(): String = "Lamp: name=$name, bulbs=$bulbs, shade=$shade" } KSP lets the processor write the file, because KSP doesn't check what's in new files. The Kotlin compiler then meets two classes with one name: === repack Lamp itself: COMPILATION_ERROR e: LampExtra.kt:3:12 Redeclaration: data class Lamp : Any e: Lamp.kt:6:12 Redeclaration: data class Lamp : Any === add a new card next to it: OK desk.inventoryCard() returns: Lamp: name=desk lamp, bulbs=1, shade=null That's one lamp too many. A processor's outputs are always new declarations next to yours: an extension function, a builder class, a list. Java's annotation processors have the same limit, which is why the Java twin is a separate LampCards class. Some jobs need the class itself to change. A compiler plugin is a jar that the Kotlin compiler loads to change what it writes into class files, and lesson 6 of the Advanced Kotlin track met three. all-open removes final from classes a framework must subclass. no-arg adds a constructor that Kotlin code can't see. kotlinx.serialization writes a serializer inside each @Serializable class and a serializer() function on its companion. No processor can do any of those, because each one changes a class you wrote. In the picture, that's the mover who repacks the box. A plugin can do more, and it costs more to trust: kt-06's whole lesson was that the class you read is not the class the JVM loads. The ecosystem shows the same line from the other side. Arrow Optics, which generates helpers for reading and copying nested data, still ships a KSP processor, but its docs say "Since version 2.3.0, we recommend using the compiler plug-in". With KSP, "you need to have a companion object declaration in each class, even if it's empty. This is due to limitations in KSP itself": the processor can add functions to a companion, but it can't create the companion. Koin is a library that builds a program's objects and hands them out. Its docs say koin-ksp-compiler "is deprecated and will be removed in a future Koin version", and its annotations are now processed by the Koin Compiler Plugin. Kotlin DataFrame deprecated its KSP plugin the same way. All three pages were checked in November 2026. If the job is to change a class, it's a compiler plugin's job. If it's to write a new file next to the class, it's KSP's. The figure puts the choice in three questions. The last box is for a helper we haven't met properly yet: the one who reads only Java. Repack a box, then write a card next to it Run RepackDemo. Read both errors: which files do they name, and at which lines? Open labs/ksp/build/kctfork/demo/l09-repack/ksp/sources/kotlin/movemate/LampExtra.kt. The second compile cleared the folder, so it holds the extension function. Which line would a Java caller write to call it? RepackDemo.kt package workbench.ksp.l09.demo import com.google.devtools.ksp.buildApiF